WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.400
One gene has prevailed at its hub.

2
00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:09.220
What keeps that choice stable, so no new gene escapes later?

3
00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:12.450
Competition helps one gene prevail.

4
00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:15.190
Holding the choice is a different question.

5
00:00:16.233 --> 00:00:17.933
Part of the answer is the lock.

6
00:00:17.933 --> 00:00:21.933
In the model, the receptor the winning gene makes closes it.

7
00:00:22.783 --> 00:00:27.103
So what should a neuron do if its chosen gene cannot make a working receptor?

8
00:00:33.233 --> 00:00:37.053
In the model, the lock does not close, so the neuron should choose again.

9
00:00:37.993 --> 00:00:42.373
It does. Such neurons often express a second receptor gene.

10
00:00:45.883 --> 00:00:47.063
The converse agrees.

11
00:00:48.123 --> 00:00:52.763
Neurons given a working receptor transgene early rarely express their own receptor

12
00:00:52.763 --> 00:00:53.763
genes.

13
00:00:54.283 --> 00:00:56.563
So the lock depends on receptor protein.

14
00:00:57.567 --> 00:01:02.127
Dalton, Lyons and Lomvardas traced the lock to the unfolded protein response.

15
00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:08.090
That is how a cell reacts to misfolded protein in its endoplasmic reticulum.

16
00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:10.510
Step one is the alarm.

17
00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:13.650
Receptor expression engages the response.

18
00:01:14.733 --> 00:01:16.173
Step two is the relay.

19
00:01:17.173 --> 00:01:22.973
PERK-dependent phosphorylation of eIF2α slows most translation but favours a few

20
00:01:22.973 --> 00:01:23.973
messages.

21
00:01:24.533 --> 00:01:28.613
One favoured message makes the transcription factor ATF5.

22
00:01:29.667 --> 00:01:31.167
Step three is the order.

23
00:01:32.127 --> 00:01:35.467
ATF5 turns on the gene for adenylyl cyclase 3.

24
00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:37.970
Step four has two effects.

25
00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:42.170
The cyclase helps turn down Lsd1, the candidate key.

26
00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:46.210
With the key withdrawn, later escapes become unlikely.

27
00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:51.250
The cyclase also relieves the stress, so the alarm switches itself off.

28
00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:58.490
The lock works on top of the earlier silence, and alongside the RNA step that quiets

29
00:01:58.490 --> 00:01:59.490
rivals.

30
00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.240
Experiments in mutant mice put these steps in order.

31
00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:07.660
They do not show how a receptor activates PERK.

32
00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:11.710
This cyclase also carries odour signals in the cilia.

33
00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:15.330
But in this model, receptor protein sets the lock.

34
00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:18.440
You can look at each neuron only once.

35
00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:20.800
How could you tell it had switched receptors?

36
00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:28.240
Leave a permanent mark.

37
00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:34.060
In lineage tracing, a recombinase made from one receptor's locus marks the neuron

38
00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:35.060
for good.

39
00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:41.730
A marked neuron that now expresses a different receptor has switched.

40
00:02:42.783 --> 00:02:48.083
In two-day-old pups lacking the cyclase, the ratio of singly to doubly labelled

41
00:02:48.083 --> 00:02:50.283
neurons was about twice the control.

42
00:02:51.283 --> 00:02:53.123
That points to a less stable choice.

43
00:02:53.963 --> 00:02:55.823
Was the cyclase step slowed, or lost?

44
00:02:56.743 --> 00:02:58.283
This ratio alone cannot tell.

45
00:02:59.267 --> 00:03:00.887
A lock need not be perfect.

46
00:03:01.847 --> 00:03:05.627
Other steps, such as the RNA competition, may still limit expression.

47
00:03:06.767 --> 00:03:10.867
So receptor protein sets off a stress relay that turns down the key.

48
00:03:11.667 --> 00:03:12.887
That helps lock in the winner.
